        "original_id": "b7e6b1d3-8691-5366-ae34-ffd7e4b2707e",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Heckscher State Parkway, Great River, Islip Terrace, Long Island, New York, Heckscher State Park, Fire Island, Great South Bay, Atlantic Ocean, New York City\n- Topics: road routes, geography\n\nFacts:\n- The Heckscher State Parkway, formerly known as the Heckscher Spur, is an 8.24-mile parkway on Long Island in New York, United States.\n- At Islip Terrace, the Heckscher State Parkway turns south, crosses NY 27, and ends at the toll barrier for Heckscher State Park in Great River.\n- Great River is a suburban hamlet and census-designated place in the Town of Islip in Suffolk County, New York.\n- Great River is about 50 miles east of New York City, or about 55 miles by driving distance.\n- Great River is on the South Shore of Long Island, adjoins the Great South Bay, and is protected from the Atlantic Ocean by Fire Island.",
